Z||000004||theogre||09-29-1999||11:54 PM||||If you are buying them because they don't create volume in a land fill fine. If you are using them thinking they give you anything beyond a fractional HP boost..... save your money. No air filter increases power by itself. Only the Cone filters have been shown to improve laminar air flow when used with some configurations of intake systems. OEM style K&N do not improve laminar flow over OEM style paper. (Laminar flow is flow with little or no turbulance.) air flow thru OEM style filters, regardless of filter media, is dependant almost completely on the desing of the filter can/box and the plumbing of the system.<P>I strongly recomend you look at all the filters and the testing methods used. Look carefully at what K&N's "million mile" waranty really covers. As I understand K&N it only covers the filter.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-30-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||BlackGT||09-30-1999||12:42 PM||||Hey Guys if you are willing to give Pep Boys<BR>or Auto Zone $30 for a paper air filter then might I interest you some bridges I have for sale? Seriously $30 I spent 3.93 at Wal Mart for a FRAM OEM filter and it works as well as<BR>the any filter on the market. There are other<BR>ways to boost power on our cars  but I doubt<BR>that a $30 filter is one of them, Just my 0.02!  ||||reg||6
Z||000006||mwbackus||09-30-1999||01:59 PM||||You guys all missed the point here.  Its the sticker that increases the horsepower, the filter itself has very little effect!!  This has been well illustrated in the book "Rice Boy Rad Mods", (see chapter 1: "Precision sticker application for optimal hp").<BR>;-)<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||7
